\cf3\highlight4\b Q. Name 5 chemical reactions that are take place in biological systems?\highlight0\par
\cf0\b0 The reactions going on in biological systems can be categorized into 5 types : \par
1. Reactions that make or break carbon-carbon bonds \par
2. Internal rearrangements, isomerizations, and eliminations \par
3. Free-radical reactions \par
4. Group transfer \par
5. Oxidation-reduction reactions\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What is enzyme inhibitor ? Discuss 2 classes of enzyme inhibitors? Explain.(5)\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
An enzyme inhibitor is a molecule that binds to an enzyme and decreases its activity.\par
Two broad classes of enzyme inhibitors: \par
\f2\bullet\f0  1. Reversible \par
\f2\bullet\f0  2. Irreversible\par
\cf3\b Reversible Enzyme Inhibitor \cf0 :\par
\b0 Reversible inhibitors typically bind to enzymes through noncovalent bonds. The two most commonly encountered types of reversible inhibition are ; \par
\cf3\b\lang1033 C\lang9 ompetitive Enzyme Inhibitor \lang1033 :\cf0\b0\par
Competitive inhibitors resemble the substrate and compete for binding to the active site of the enzyme. This type of inhibition occurs when the inhibitor binds reversibly to the same site that the substrate would normally occupy i.e. active site and therefore, competes with the substrate for that site. A competitive inhibitor does not decrease Vmax.\lang9\par
\cf3\b\lang1033 N\lang9 on\lang1033 -\lang9 competitive\lang1033  \lang9 Enzyme Inhibitor :\cf0\par
\b0 Inhibitors bind enzymes at sites distinct from the substrate-binding site and generally bear little or \lang1033 n\lang9 o structural resemblance to the substrate.\lang1033  It cannot be overcome by increasing the concentration of substrate. Thus, non-competitive inhibitors decrease the Vmax of the reaction.  The noncompetitive inhibitor in effect lowers the concentration of the active enzyme and therefore decreases the Vmax of the enzyme. \par
\cf3\b Irr\lang9 eversible Enzyme Inhibitor :\cf0\par
\b0 Irreversible inhibitors bind to enzymes through covalent bonds.\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What is enone ? (2)\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
An enone is an beta-unsaturated chemical compound or functional group consisting of a conjugated system of an alkene and a ketone. e.g : Methyl Vinyl Ketone(CH2=CHCOCH3) \par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. Difference between Oxidation and Reduction ? (5)\highlight0\par
Definition\par
Oxidation\cf0 : \b0 Oxidation can be defined as the loss of electrons from an atom, molecule or an ion.\par
\cf3\b Reduction: \cf0\b0 Reduction can be defined as the gain of electrons from an atom, molecule or an ion.\par
\cf3\b Change of Oxidation State\par
Oxidation: \cf0\b0 The oxidation state increases in oxidation.\par
\cf3\b Reduction\cf0 :  \b0 The oxidation state decreases in reduction.\par
\cf3\b Exchange of Electrons\par
Oxidation: \cf0\b0 Oxidation reactions release electrons to the surrounding.\par
\cf3\b Reduction: \cf0\b0 Reduction reactions obtain electrons from the surrounding.\par
\cf3\b Change of Electrical Charge\par
Oxidation: \cf0\b0 Oxidation causes the increase of positive charge of a chemical species.\par
\cf3\b Reduction: \cf0\b0 Reduction causes the increase of negative charge of a chemical species.\par
\cf3\b Chemical Species Involved\cf0\par
\cf3 Oxidation: \cf0\b0 Oxidation occurs in reducing agents.\par
\cf3\b Reduction\cf0 : \b0 Reduction occurs in oxidizing agents.\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What is equilibrium constant K? (2)\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
The equilibrium constant, K, expresses the relationship between products and reactants of a reaction at equilibrium with respect to a specific unit.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\cf3\b OR\cf0\b0\par

