\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What are the origin of Phytanic Acid ?\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
Phytol is the alcohol moiety of the chlorophyll molecule and, as an integral part of chlorophyll, it is abundantly present in plants and even in the marine environment. \f2 Phytol can be released from chlorophyll by the action of bacteria present in the rumen of\f0  \f2 ruminant animals. The resulting free phytol can be converted to phytanic acid in the rumen of animals\f0 .\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What is acidosis ?\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
 The increased blood levels of acetoacetate and D-betA\'02hydroxybutyrate lower the blood pH, causing the condition known as acidosis. \par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What is  ketosis ?\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
Extreme acidosis can lead to coma and in some cases death. Ketone bodies in the blood and urine of individuals with untreated diabetes can reach extraordinary levels\f2\emdash a blood concentration of 90 mg/100 mL (compared with a normal level of ,3 mg/100 mL) and urinary excretion of 5,000 mg/24 hr (compared with a normal rate of 125 mg/24 hr). This condition is called ketosis.\par
\cf3\highlight4\b\f0 Q. What is Refsum disease ?\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
 A disorder resulting from a genetic defect in phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase, leads to very high blood levels of phytanic acid and severe neurological problems, including blindness and deafness. \f2 Phytanic acid accumulates in patients suffering from a peroxisome biogenesis disorder, which includes Zellweger Syndrome, Neonatal\f0  \f2 Adrenoleukodystrophy, and Infantile Refsum Disease. Formic acid and not CO2\f0  \f2 is the primary reaction product of phytanic acid oxidation. The amount of formic acid was shown to be about 9-fold higher as compared to CO2\f0 .\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What is origin of \f2 Adipic acid\f0  ?\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
Adipic acid or hexanedioic acid (6 carbon chain) is the organic compound with the formula (CH2)4(COOH)2. From an industrial perspective, it is the most important dicarboxylic acid. \f2 About 2.5 billion kilograms of this white crystalline powder are produced annually, mainly as a precursor for the production of nylon.\par
\cf3\highlight4\b\f0 Q. Define  Isozymes ?\cf0\b0\par
\highlight0 Two or more enzymes that catalyze the same reaction but are encoded by different genes is called isozymes.\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. Write disadvantage of having several activities on the same polypeptide ?\cf0\b0\par
\highlight0 The disadvantage of having several activities on the same polypeptide is that the longer the polypeptide chain, the greater is the probability of a mistake in its synthesis: a single incorrect amino acid in the chain may make all the enzyme activities in that chain useless. \par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. Define Glyoxysomes ?\cf0\b0\par
\highlight0 Plant peroxisomes and glyoxysomes are similar in structure and function. \f2 Glyoxysomes are specialized peroxisomes found in plants particularly in the fat storage tissues of germinating seeds\f0  \f2 and also in filamentous fungi. Seeds that contain fats and oils include corn, soybean, sunflower, peanut and pumpkin. \par
\cf3\highlight4\b\f0 Q. Which human diseases is due to the peroxisomes ?\par
\highlight0 Zellweger syndrome :\par
\cf0\b0 Individuals  with Zellweger syndrome are unable to make peroxisomes and therefore lack all the metabolism unique to that organelle. \par
\cf3\b\f2 XALD\f0  :\cf0\b0\par
\f2 In X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (XALD), peroxisomes fail to oxidize very-long-chain fatty acids, apparently for lack of a functional transporter for these fatty acids in the peroxisomal membrane. XALD affects young boys before the age of 10 years, causing loss of vision, behavioral disturbances, and death within a few years. \par
\cf3\highlight4\b\f0 Q. What is Transcription Factors ?\cf0\b0\par
\highlight0  A transcription factor (TF) or sequence-specific DNA-binding factor is a protein that controls the rate of transcription of genetic information from DNA to messenger RNA, by binding to a specific DNA sequence.\par
