\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\highlight4\f0 Q. What is Titration ?\par
\cf0\highlight0\b0 Titration is used to determine the amount of an acid in a given solution. \f2 A measured volume of the acid is titrated with a solution of a strong base, usually sodium hydroxide (NaOH), of known concentration\f0 .\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What are buffer solution ?\b0\par
\cf0\highlight0\f2 Buffers are aqueous systems that tend to resist changes in pH when small amounts of acid (H+) or base (OH-) are added\f0 . \f2 A buffer system consists of a weak acid (the proton donor) and its conjugate base (the proton acceptor)\f0 .\cf3\par
\b Examples of Buffer solution :\b0\par
\cf0  Acetic acid and A\f2 cetate ion\f0 .\par
\cf3\highlight4\b Q. What is R in the equation of equilibrium constant? Also write its value.\cf0\highlight0\b0\par
R is the gas constant in the equation of equilibrium constant with a value of \cf3 8.314 J K-1mol-1.\par
\highlight4\b Q. What are the sources of fatty acids in the cells ?\par
\cf0\highlight0\b0 Fatty acids are released from triglycerides stored in fat cells which is Adipose tissues or adipocytes. \par
